00:00:00.499 --> 00:00:01.590
SETH VARGOS: Welcome back.

00:00:01.590 --> 00:00:02.665
Next up we have Ahmet.

00:00:02.665 --> 00:00:05.040
Ahmet, why don't you tell us
a little bit of what you do?

00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:06.270
AHMET ALP BALKAN: Sure, Seth.

00:00:06.270 --> 00:00:07.110
My name is Ahmet.

00:00:07.110 --> 00:00:09.390
I'm a developer programs
engineer at Google Cloud.

00:00:09.390 --> 00:00:11.680
I work on Google
Kubernetes engine,

00:00:11.680 --> 00:00:12.930
[INAUDIBLE] project and so on.

00:00:12.930 --> 00:00:13.805
SETH VARGOS: Awesome.

00:00:13.805 --> 00:00:16.847
So you've been working in
Kubernetes for quite a while.

00:00:16.847 --> 00:00:18.180
I've been following your career.

00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:19.590
What's one of the
biggest pain points

00:00:19.590 --> 00:00:21.730
that you see in the Kubernetes
ecosystem right now?

00:00:21.730 --> 00:00:21.870
AHMET ALP BALKAN: Yeah.

00:00:21.870 --> 00:00:23.820
So my primary audiences
are developers,

00:00:23.820 --> 00:00:25.980
and I think developers deal
with these command line

00:00:25.980 --> 00:00:27.210
tools want not a lot.

00:00:27.210 --> 00:00:30.300
So I'm seeing that in some
inner iteration circles,

00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:32.860
there's a lot of developer
cycles wasted, if you will.

00:00:32.860 --> 00:00:36.090
That is like going towards
just these simple things

00:00:36.090 --> 00:00:37.830
around tools like Kube control.

00:00:37.830 --> 00:00:41.190
So I like to develop tools and
shortcuts around Kube control.

00:00:41.190 --> 00:00:42.390
That's my personal passion.

00:00:42.390 --> 00:00:44.670
SETH VARGOS: And Kube
control is like one

00:00:44.670 --> 00:00:47.275
of the main ways in which
people submit everything

00:00:47.275 --> 00:00:48.150
to Kubernetes, right?

00:00:48.150 --> 00:00:48.495
AHMET ALP BALKAN: Yeah.

00:00:48.495 --> 00:00:50.891
SETH VARGOS: So what are some
of the problems around Kube

00:00:50.891 --> 00:00:51.390
control?

00:00:51.390 --> 00:00:52.484
Or where can we expand it?

00:00:52.484 --> 00:00:54.150
Or where are people
hitting pain points?

00:00:54.150 --> 00:00:55.860
AHMET ALP BALKAN: So there's
like an entire open source

00:00:55.860 --> 00:00:58.260
special interest group, Sig
CLI, in Kubernetes that's

00:00:58.260 --> 00:00:59.950
dealing with that problem.

00:00:59.950 --> 00:01:02.910
One of the areas that they
propose improvement is that

00:01:02.910 --> 00:01:06.010
they cannot possibly implement
everything that people want.

00:01:06.010 --> 00:01:08.280
So they wanted to provide
some extension capability

00:01:08.280 --> 00:01:09.420
around kubectl.

00:01:09.420 --> 00:01:11.460
So they basically
said, hey, why don't we

00:01:11.460 --> 00:01:13.320
provide in plugin
systems so that people

00:01:13.320 --> 00:01:16.070
can write their own
programs around kubectl,

00:01:16.070 --> 00:01:18.665
and they can invoke them through
the kubectl command line.

00:01:18.665 --> 00:01:20.790
SETH VARGOS: Have you
written any plugins yourself?

00:01:20.790 --> 00:01:21.630
AHMET ALP BALKAN:
Yeah, I've been playing

00:01:21.630 --> 00:01:23.220
with some plugins myself.

00:01:23.220 --> 00:01:25.230
One thing that actually
I've been working on

00:01:25.230 --> 00:01:27.360
is a plugin manager called Krew.

00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:31.795
So this is basically
[INAUDIBLE] Mac OS and this--

00:01:31.795 --> 00:01:35.764
it's Kubernetes K, so {} There
is a nautical theme there,

00:01:35.764 --> 00:01:36.680
if you can catch that.

00:01:36.680 --> 00:01:39.284
SETH VARGOS: So
K-R-E-W, not C-R-E-W.

00:01:39.284 --> 00:01:40.200
AHMET ALP BALKAN: Yes.

00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:40.950
Yes, that's right.

00:01:40.950 --> 00:01:43.560
So this is a project that
we've been working on

00:01:43.560 --> 00:01:44.280
at Google Cloud.

00:01:44.280 --> 00:01:47.340
This actually-- this project
was started by my intern

00:01:47.340 --> 00:01:49.270
this summer, Luke Pritchard.

00:01:49.270 --> 00:01:50.810
He's [INAUDIBLE] on GitHub.

00:01:50.810 --> 00:01:53.070
So he's a super cool
guy, and w started

00:01:53.070 --> 00:01:54.150
this project called Krew.

00:01:54.150 --> 00:01:58.320
So what Krew does is it's a
plugin manager for a kubectl.

00:01:58.320 --> 00:01:59.950
So if you want to
discover plugins,

00:01:59.950 --> 00:02:02.160
if you want to install
plugins, you can go to Krew,

00:02:02.160 --> 00:02:04.894
install Krew onto your
computer, and then from there

00:02:04.894 --> 00:02:07.560
you can discover new plugins and
just bring them to your machine

00:02:07.560 --> 00:02:08.880
and then use it with kubectl.

00:02:08.880 --> 00:02:10.439
SETH VARGOS: So I
assume it's open source.

00:02:10.439 --> 00:02:10.979
AHMET ALP BALKAN: Yes.

00:02:10.979 --> 00:02:11.800
This is open source.

00:02:11.800 --> 00:02:12.930
If we haven't
announced it a lot,

00:02:12.930 --> 00:02:15.550
I guess this is probably the
biggest announcement so far.

00:02:15.550 --> 00:02:16.810
SETH VARGOS: You heard
it here first, people.

00:02:16.810 --> 00:02:17.768
AHMET ALP BALKAN: Yeah.

00:02:17.768 --> 00:02:21.540
And then right now we're in the
process of talking to Sig CLI

00:02:21.540 --> 00:02:24.930
to perhaps donate this project
to Kubernetes open source

00:02:24.930 --> 00:02:27.369
community and make it
a non-Google project.

00:02:27.369 --> 00:02:29.535
SETH VARGOS: Well, so let's
get technical for a bit.

00:02:29.535 --> 00:02:32.400
Since this is the announcement,
how does Krew find plugins?

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:34.037
Is there a registry, GitHub?

00:02:34.037 --> 00:02:34.870
How do we find them?

00:02:34.870 --> 00:02:36.828
AHMET ALP BALKAN: So we
have a central registry

00:02:36.828 --> 00:02:38.120
on GitHub W Outbreak currently.

00:02:38.120 --> 00:02:39.702
Anyone who would do
all those plugins,

00:02:39.702 --> 00:02:41.790
we just need those people
to package their plugins

00:02:41.790 --> 00:02:44.357
into, say, something like
a tarball or zip file,

00:02:44.357 --> 00:02:46.440
and then they write a
manifest file describing how

00:02:46.440 --> 00:02:47.850
the plugin should be installed.

00:02:47.850 --> 00:02:50.190
And then they submit
this plugin manifest

00:02:50.190 --> 00:02:52.980
file to the central
repository that we have,

00:02:52.980 --> 00:02:55.480
and then from there everyone
can discover those plugins.

00:02:55.480 --> 00:02:57.430
So if you're shopping
for some, let's say,

00:02:57.430 --> 00:02:59.220
a plug to read the
logs, you don't

00:02:59.220 --> 00:03:01.530
have to go to Google
Search and like search what

00:03:01.530 --> 00:03:03.100
are the plugins that I can use.

00:03:03.100 --> 00:03:05.250
So you can just come to
Krew and search for logs,

00:03:05.250 --> 00:03:06.540
and then we'll show
you all the plugins

00:03:06.540 --> 00:03:07.680
that are about log reading.

00:03:07.680 --> 00:03:09.210
SETH VARGOS: Well,
so let's go back.

00:03:09.210 --> 00:03:10.800
Let's say Krew doesn't exist.

00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:13.860
How do I get a plugin into
kubectl today without Krew?

00:03:13.860 --> 00:03:15.151
AHMET ALP BALKAN: Yeah.

00:03:15.151 --> 00:03:16.650
Well, first of all,
you need to find

00:03:16.650 --> 00:03:18.719
that a plugin that you're
looking for exists.

00:03:18.719 --> 00:03:20.510
I think that's one of
the biggest problems,

00:03:20.510 --> 00:03:21.780
and that's one of
the primary reasons

00:03:21.780 --> 00:03:22.863
that we just started Krew.

00:03:22.863 --> 00:03:25.510
Discoverability is one of the
largest problems out there.

00:03:25.510 --> 00:03:27.240
So after you find
the plugin, you

00:03:27.240 --> 00:03:28.890
need to download
to your package,

00:03:28.890 --> 00:03:31.350
and then you need to name
it in a specific phase.

00:03:31.350 --> 00:03:33.270
So let's say you have
a plugin called food,

00:03:33.270 --> 00:03:36.450
so you need to name
it kubectl-food

00:03:36.450 --> 00:03:39.001
and then put it into
somewhere in your path.

00:03:39.001 --> 00:03:41.250
And if you put it in your
path and everything is good,

00:03:41.250 --> 00:03:44.160
you can invoke it by
saying kubectl space food.

00:03:44.160 --> 00:03:46.300
So that's how you install
and set up plugins.

00:03:46.300 --> 00:03:47.490
SETH VARGOS: That sounds
like a lot of magic.

00:03:47.490 --> 00:03:48.948
AHMET ALP BALKAN:
So imagine you're

00:03:48.948 --> 00:03:50.760
installing 10 to
15 plugins, and you

00:03:50.760 --> 00:03:53.220
have to keep them up to date
because all these plugins will

00:03:53.220 --> 00:03:54.360
have newer versions.

00:03:54.360 --> 00:03:56.820
So that's another thing that
Krew solves really well.

00:03:56.820 --> 00:03:58.290
If you manage the
plugin lifecycle

00:03:58.290 --> 00:04:01.080
and we can bring the
updates automatically,

00:04:01.080 --> 00:04:03.730
you basically say, hey,
kubectl Krew update,

00:04:03.730 --> 00:04:05.940
and we basically update
all these plugins

00:04:05.940 --> 00:04:07.255
if they have newer versions.

00:04:07.255 --> 00:04:08.130
SETH VARGOS: Awesome.

00:04:08.130 --> 00:04:09.790
So this thing
sounds really cool.

00:04:09.790 --> 00:04:12.580
One, where can people get more
information, and two, what's

00:04:12.580 --> 00:04:13.830
the future look like for Krew?

00:04:13.830 --> 00:04:14.788
AHMET ALP BALKAN: Yeah.

00:04:14.788 --> 00:04:17.512
So people can go
to GitHub.com/Googl

00:04:17.512 --> 00:04:21.870
eContainerTools/Krew with
K. Feel free to try it out.

00:04:21.870 --> 00:04:24.090
I think we're on something
like version 0.2.

00:04:24.090 --> 00:04:25.811
This is still very early days.

00:04:25.811 --> 00:04:27.060
So far it's been working fine.

00:04:27.060 --> 00:04:29.722
We have over, I think,
almost 20 plugins.

00:04:29.722 --> 00:04:31.680
By the way, there are
not a lot of plugins yet,

00:04:31.680 --> 00:04:33.960
so we also want people to
think about plugin ideas

00:04:33.960 --> 00:04:35.580
and what to develop.

00:04:35.580 --> 00:04:39.250
But also we want to donate
Krew to Kubernetes project,

00:04:39.250 --> 00:04:43.200
so we want it to be a Kubernetes
Sig CLI project that perhaps

00:04:43.200 --> 00:04:45.660
someday eventually
rolls into kubectl,

00:04:45.660 --> 00:04:49.830
so that you can just say
kubectl plugins install food.

00:04:49.830 --> 00:04:52.950
So we want to go to a world
where these sort of things

00:04:52.950 --> 00:04:54.975
are easily discoverable
and manageable.

00:04:54.975 --> 00:04:55.950
SETH VARGOS: Awesome.

00:04:55.950 --> 00:04:57.075
Thank you very much, Ahmet.

00:04:57.075 --> 00:04:58.700
AHMET ALP BALKAN:
Thank you, Seth.

